<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Wen of Sui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/541">541</a>–<a href="/years/604">604</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Yang Jian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Wen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>, born Yang Jian, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> official who seized power and founded the Sui dynasty in 581. He conquered the southern Chen dynasty in 589, reuniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after nearly three centuries of division. He built the new capital Daxing, reformed law and administration and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> works continued by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui.jpg" alt="Emperor Wen of Sui" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yan Li-pen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sui_Wendi_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yang Jian belonged to the northwestern Chinese military aristocracy, in the Yang clan of Hongnong, which had Han origins but had intermarried with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> for generations. His father Yang Zhong followed the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>. Yang Jian was born in a Buddhist temple, and a Buddhist nun, Zhixian, raised him in his early years. At 14 he entered the army under Yuwen Tai, and in 557 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dugu">Dugu Qieluo</a>. In 573 his daughter Yang Lihua became the wife of the crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuan-of-northern-zhou">Yuwen Yun</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> emperor Xuan died in 580, Yang, as his father-in-law, took power as regent. General Yuchi Jiong rose against him, and within 68 days he was defeated, as was Wang Qian. Yang then began to eliminate the Yuwen clan and in spring 581 had Emperor Jing yield the throne, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>. He reorganised the government into five main bureaus, had Pei Zheng simplify the penal code, a reform later largely accepted by subsequent dynasties, and built a new capital, Daxing, to which he moved in 583. He also built the Guangtong Canal in 584.</p><p>In 588 he announced a campaign against the southern Chen dynasty under his son Yang Guang, and in spring 589 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> army captured the Chen capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and took the Chen emperor captive, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-and-southern-dynasties">Northern and Southern dynasties</a> period. In the north he worked to divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a>, who by the end of his reign had split into an eastern and a western khaganate, and a campaign against Goguryeo in 598 ended in heavy losses. In 592 he reduced taxes and had land redistributed to poor farmers, and it was said that enough food was stored for 50 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 600 Emperor Wen deposed his eldest son Yang Yong as crown prince and replaced him with Yang Guang. Empress Dugu died in 602. He died at Renshou Palace in 604 and was buried at the Tailing mausoleum. How he died is controversial: most traditional historians hold that Yang Guang had him killed, and Yang Guang took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>.</p><p>The historian Sima Guang described him as cautious, hard-working and frugal, who lowered the burden of labour and tax on his people, but also as suspicious of his officials and even of his own sons. According to him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> rolls grew from under four million households to almost nine million during the reign. As a Buddhist, Wen encouraged the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> through the state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Wen of Sui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its reforms reversed the devolution of power to local strongmen; the equal-field system outlived the dynasty to the mid-8th century, and Ray Huang has called these reforms the 'infrastructure of a regenerated empire' whose agricultural resources and peasant manpower <b>Yang Jian</b> inherited to unite China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 577 the Xianbei-led Northern Zhou conquered the Northern Qi and reunified the north, and the general <b>Yang Jian</b> became regent at the Zhou court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Yang Jian</b>, an official of the Northern Zhou, seized power and founded the Sui dynasty as Emperor Wen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-4">4 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>When the child Emperor Jing succeeded, the regent <b>Yang Jian</b>, father of Emperor Xuan's empress, crushed opposition and took the throne in 581 as founder of the Sui Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a></li><li><blockquote>In 587 <b>Emperor Wen of Sui</b> summoned the third ruler, Xiao Cong, to Chang'an and abolished the state; some of its officials fled to the Chen dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-liang-555-587">Western Liang (555-587)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baoji">Baoji</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=581" aria-label="Chang'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Emperor Wen of Sui"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4218 4594h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4218 4594h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=581">What was here in 581 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
